1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,358
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,358 --> 00:00:09,155
This is chapter five: migration strategy, from Java 8 to 25.

3
00:00:09,605 --> 00:00:13,574
Upgrading the JDK is rarely hard because of any one change.

4
00:00:13,574 --> 00:00:21,850
It's hard because teams treat it as one change, when it's several different kinds of breakage stacked on top of each other.

5
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:24,324
Two failure patterns.

6
00:00:24,324 --> 00:00:27,921
The stall: a team has been on Java 8 since 2016.

7
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:35,565
Every year someone proposes upgrading, the estimate is a quarter, maybe, and it loses to feature work.

8
00:00:35,565 --> 00:00:42,910
Meanwhile libraries raise their baselines, nothing can be updated, and vulnerabilities accumulate.

9
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:47,841
And the big bang: a team goes from 8 to 21 in one branch.

10
00:00:47,841 --> 00:00:55,072
Three weeks in, there are four hundred changed files and failing tests nobody can attribute.

11
00:00:55,072 --> 00:01:00,811
Was it the JDK, a framework upgrade, a removed API, or a changed default?

12
00:01:01,261 --> 00:01:05,591
Both come from the same mistake: treating the upgrade as one change.

13
00:01:06,491 --> 00:01:09,176
There are three kinds of breakage.

14
00:01:09,176 --> 00:01:11,940
Removed APIs, like JAXB in Java 11.

15
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:16,283
Those fail at compile time or startup, so they're loud.

16
00:01:16,733 --> 00:01:22,069
Encapsulated internals, like reflection into Unsafe, blocked in 17.

17
00:01:22,069 --> 00:01:27,165
Those fail at runtime, but only on the code path that uses them.

18
00:01:27,615 --> 00:01:32,303
And changed defaults: locale data, the default garbage collector.

19
00:01:32,303 --> 00:01:33,529
Those are silent.

20
00:01:33,529 --> 00:01:36,198
You find out when a customer notices.

21
00:01:36,198 --> 00:01:40,309
That's why a migration needs testing, not just compiling.

22
00:01:40,759 --> 00:01:44,771
And most of the work is in dependencies, not your code.

23
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:47,324
Your code rarely touches internals.

24
00:01:47,324 --> 00:01:51,846
Your old bytecode library, XML stack and mocking framework do.

25
00:01:51,846 --> 00:01:58,849
So the estimate depends on how far behind your dependencies are, not on how big the codebase is.

26
00:01:59,749 --> 00:02:04,343
Before estimating anything, spend a day on an assessment.

27
00:02:04,343 --> 00:02:09,258
jdeps lists internal API use across the whole dependency set.

28
00:02:09,258 --> 00:02:12,079
jdeprscan lists deprecated API use.

29
00:02:12,079 --> 00:02:16,914
The versions plugin shows how far behind each dependency is.

30
00:02:16,914 --> 00:02:21,829
And try starting the app on JDK 17 without changing anything.

31
00:02:22,279 --> 00:02:31,211
The output splits cleanly into your code, usually a handful of places, and dependencies, usually most of it, fixed by upgrading.

32
00:02:31,211 --> 00:02:33,095
That split is the estimate.

33
00:02:33,995 --> 00:02:38,650
The first step, from 8 to 17, in order, each as its own PR.

34
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:43,147
First, upgrade dependencies while you're still on Java 8.

35
00:02:43,147 --> 00:02:51,510
Most libraries that support 17 still support 8, so this removes most of the risk without touching the JDK.

36
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:57,911
Second, add the removed Java EE modules back as ordinary dependencies.

37
00:02:57,911 --> 00:03:03,523
That's the fix for NoClassDefFoundError on javax dot xml dot bind.

38
00:03:03,973 --> 00:03:08,372
Third, switch the build to release 17 and fix the compile errors.

39
00:03:08,372 --> 00:03:09,861
There are usually few.

40
00:03:10,311 --> 00:03:14,267
Fourth, start the application and collect the reflection failures.

41
00:03:14,267 --> 00:03:18,823
For each one, find the library, and look for a version that doesn't need it.

42
00:03:19,273 --> 00:03:21,793
Fifth, verify the behaviour changes.

43
00:03:21,793 --> 00:03:23,753
This is the step teams skip.

44
00:03:24,203 --> 00:03:28,960
And sixth, ship Java 17 to production before starting the next step.

45
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:32,545
Here's the typical reflection failure.

46
00:03:32,545 --> 00:03:40,036
A library is reaching into a private field of ArrayList, and java dot base won't open java dot util to it.

47
00:03:40,486 --> 00:03:43,605
When no fixed version exists yet, add the flag.

48
00:03:43,605 --> 00:03:50,107
But put it in an argument file, with a comment naming the library and the version that removes it.

49
00:03:50,557 --> 00:03:57,915
Add opens works per package, not per class, so it opens all of java dot util to all classpath code.

50
00:03:57,915 --> 00:04:04,232
Treat each one as a ticket with an owner and an upgrade target, not as configuration.

51
00:04:05,132 --> 00:04:09,240
Java 9 switched the default locale data to CLDR.

52
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:16,941
The same UK medium date that printed twenty dash Sep dash 2026 now prints twenty Sep 2026.

53
00:04:16,941 --> 00:04:18,139
Nothing fails.

54
00:04:18,139 --> 00:04:21,477
A downstream parser breaks weeks later.

55
00:04:21,477 --> 00:04:27,809
If a JDK-formatted date crosses a system boundary, assert on it in a test.

56
00:04:28,259 --> 00:04:39,803
Others: the default collector moved from Parallel to G1, which is better for latency but can cost a batch job throughput, without anyone changing a flag.

57
00:04:39,803 --> 00:04:43,047
CMS was removed, so old flags stop startup.

58
00:04:43,047 --> 00:04:44,254
Nashorn is gone.

59
00:04:44,254 --> 00:04:46,819
And old TLS versions are disabled.

60
00:04:47,719 --> 00:04:50,057
After 17, the steps are easy.

61
00:04:50,057 --> 00:04:59,730
For 21: check deprecations, Thread dot stop now throws, and remember that synchronized still pins virtual threads on 21.

62
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:06,369
For 25: compact object headers make objects smaller, so re-baseline heap sizing.

63
00:05:06,369 --> 00:05:12,093
Recheck your add-opens list, because many libraries no longer need theirs.

64
00:05:12,093 --> 00:05:16,735
And remove any workarounds for pinning, which Java 24 fixed.

65
00:05:17,185 --> 00:05:21,073
And resist adopting new features in the upgrade PR.

66
00:05:21,073 --> 00:05:29,003
A JDK upgrade should change the JDK and nothing else, or you lose the ability to attribute a regression.

67
00:05:29,903 --> 00:05:40,421
Roll out in stages: a CI matrix on both JDKs, staging, a canary for a few hours, a quarter of traffic for a day, then everything.

68
00:05:40,871 --> 00:05:43,630
Here's the property that makes it safe.

69
00:05:43,630 --> 00:05:51,201
Compiled with release 17, the same artifact runs on 17 and on 21, so rollback is just swapping the runtime.

70
00:05:51,201 --> 00:05:54,385
Raising the release flag is the one-way door.

71
00:05:54,385 --> 00:06:00,470
So move the runtime first, run it for a release cycle, and only then raise the target.

72
00:06:00,470 --> 00:06:06,343
Teams that do both together lose their cheapest rollback exactly when they need it.

73
00:06:06,793 --> 00:06:18,611
On the canary, watch GC pause distribution, memory outside the heap, p99 latency, and errors on anything that formats dates, numbers or currency.

74
00:06:19,511 --> 00:06:20,910
Production rules.

75
00:06:20,910 --> 00:06:22,966
Assess before estimating.

76
00:06:22,966 --> 00:06:26,421
Upgrade dependencies on the old JDK first.

77
00:06:26,421 --> 00:06:30,451
Step from 8 to 17 to 21 to 25, shipping each one.

78
00:06:30,451 --> 00:06:34,481
Move the runtime before raising the release flag.

79
00:06:34,481 --> 00:06:37,607
Comment every add-opens with an owner.

80
00:06:37,607 --> 00:06:42,295
And test the silent changes: locale, GC, TLS and charset.

81
00:06:42,745 --> 00:06:45,516
Never jump from 8 to 25 in one step.

82
00:06:45,516 --> 00:06:49,518
Never adopt new language features in the upgrade PR.

83
00:06:49,518 --> 00:06:52,520
Never treat add-opens as configuration.

84
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:54,675
Never trust a clean compile.

85
00:06:54,675 --> 00:06:58,447
And never skip the canary because staging passed.

86
00:06:59,347 --> 00:07:00,028
To recap.

87
00:07:00,028 --> 00:07:06,611
Breakage comes in three kinds, and the silent behaviour changes are the dangerous ones.

88
00:07:06,611 --> 00:07:09,562
The work is mostly dependency upgrades.

89
00:07:09,562 --> 00:07:14,330
Java 17 is the landing point, and after it, 21 and 25 are easy.

90
00:07:14,330 --> 00:07:20,232
And move the runtime before raising the release flag, so rollback stays cheap.

91
00:07:20,682 --> 00:07:23,187
That completes the platform section.

92
00:07:23,187 --> 00:07:27,013
Next is chapter six: lambdas and functional interfaces.

93
00:07:27,013 --> 00:07:28,405
Thanks for watching.
